{"type":"result","subtype":"success","session_id":"agent-597b5756-2915-4560-ba6b-91005f085166","duration_ms":12887,"duration_api_ms":0,"num_turns":1,"result":"A good API is **predictable**. It follows consistent conventions across endpoints — naming, error formats, pagination, authentication — so once a developer learns one part, the rest feels familiar. Resources, verbs, and status codes behave the way you'd expect, and surprising behavior is documented loudly rather than hidden in edge cases. Predictability is what lets people build confidently without constantly re-reading the docs.\n\nA good API is **honest about failure**. Errors come back with clear, machine-readable codes and human-readable messages that explain *what* went wrong and, ideally, *how to fix it*. It distinguishes client mistakes from server problems, never silently truncates or transforms data, and treats backwards compatibility as a contract — versioning changes instead of breaking callers. Rate limits, timeouts, and partial failures are surfaced explicitly, not buried.\n\nA good API is **shaped around the caller**, not the database. It exposes operations that match real workflows rather than forcing clients to stitch together five requests to do one obvious thing. Payloads are minimal but complete, defaults are sensible, and the docs include working examples — not just schemas. The best APIs feel less like a wall of endpoints and more like a well-designed library someone genuinely wanted you to use.","agent_id":"agent-597b5756-2915-4560-ba6b-91005f085166","conversation_id":"local-conv-9","run_ids":[],"usage":null,"uuid":"result-agent-597b5756-2915-4560-ba6b-91005f085166-1778797677357","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:27:57.357Z"}
